<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/snow-chimney.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/snow-chimney-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/snow-chimney.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="683" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-calculators/">Calculators</a></li><li aria-current="page">Snow &amp; Ice Roof Load Estimator</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Free estimator · nothing stored</p><h1>Roof Snow Load Calculator for Maryland Winter Storms</h1><p class="lede">After storms like the February 2010 blizzards, roof collapses and sagging ceilings followed the snow. Estimate the weight sitting on your roof — and know when to call a professional.</p></div></section>
<section class="sec tool-sec"><div class="wrap"><div class="rv">
<div class="tool" data-tool="snow">
  <div class="tool-head"><span>Snow &amp; ice roof load estimator</span><i>Illustrative — not an engineering check</i></div>
  <div class="tool-body">
    <div class="tool-in">
      <div class="row2">
        <div><label for="s-d">Snow depth on the roof (inches)</label><input id="s-d" name="depth" inputmode="decimal" value="24"></div>
        <div><label for="s-t">Snow type</label><select id="s-t" name="dens"><option value="7">Fresh, powdery (~7 lb/ft³)</option><option value="15" selected>Settled (~15 lb/ft³)</option><option value="21">Wet or packed (~21 lb/ft³)</option></select></div>
      </div>
      <div class="row2">
        <div><label for="s-i">Ice layer (inches)</label><input id="s-i" name="ice" inputmode="decimal" value="1"><span class="hint">Ice weighs about 57 lb/ft³.</span></div>
        <div><label for="s-a">Roof area (sq ft, plan view)</label><input id="s-a" name="area" inputmode="decimal" value="1400"></div>
      </div>
      <div><label for="s-c">Reference roof snow load (psf)</label><select id="s-c" name="cap"><option value="20">20 psf — older or light framing</option><option value="25" selected>25 psf — common central/eastern figure</option><option value="30">30 psf</option><option value="40">40 psf</option><option value="60">60 psf — mountain-built framing</option></select><span class="hint">Your actual design load depends on code, age and framing. Ask an engineer or your local building office.</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tool-out" aria-live="polite">
      <div class="out-big"><small>Estimated load on the roof</small><b data-o="psf">0 psf</b></div>
      <div class="stack" aria-hidden="true"><span class="s3"></span><span class="s1"></span></div>
      <div class="legend"><span><i class="s3"></i>Estimated load</span><span><i class="s1"></i>Remaining to reference load</span></div>
      <div class="out-rows">
        <div><span>Snow weight per sq ft</span><b data-o="snowpsf">0</b></div>
        <div><span>Ice weight per sq ft</span><b data-o="icepsf">0</b></div>
        <div><span>Total weight on the roof</span><b data-o="total">0 lb</b></div>
        <div><span>Share of reference load</span><b data-o="share">0%</b></div>
      </div>
      <p class="out-msg" data-o="snowmsg"></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div></div>
<p class="hint">For planning only. Results depend on the figures you enter and your policy’s terms. They are not a quote, an appraisal or legal advice.</p></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>How the estimate works</h2>
<p>Snow weight is depth (in feet) times density. Fresh powder can be under 10 lb/ft³; wet, settled snow can be twice that or more, and ice is about 57 lb/ft³. The estimator adds snow and ice to give pounds per square foot (psf), then compares the result with the reference load you choose. Two feet of settled snow is roughly 30 psf — already above a common 25 psf reference.</p>
<h2>Why Western Maryland is different</h2>
<p>Garrett and western Allegany counties see far more snow than the Bay counties, and buildings there are often framed for heavier loads. A lake cabin near Deep Creek and a 1950s rambler in Bowie can respond very differently to the same storm total.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Safety first</b>This is an illustrative estimate, not an engineering assessment. Don’t climb onto a snow-covered roof. If you see sagging, new cracks or doors that suddenly stick, leave the area and call a contractor or structural engineer.</div>
<h2>When it becomes a claim</h2>
<p>Collapse, ice-dam leaks and burst pipes are frequent winter claims, and coverage often turns on policy wording about weight of snow and ice. See <a href="/property-claim-services/snow-ice-dam-freeze-appraisal/">snow, ice dam &amp; freeze appraisal</a> for how these losses are valued.</p>
<h2>Questions about this tool</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What design snow load does my house have?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>It depends on when and where it was built and how it was framed. Your county building office or a structural engineer can tell you; the reference values here are only illustrative.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does homeowners insurance cover roof collapse from snow?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Many policies address collapse caused by the weight of snow or ice, but wording and exclusions vary. Read the collapse and ‘weight of ice, snow or sleet’ provisions and ask your insurer or an attorney.</p></div></details></div><h2>More free calculators</h2><div class="rel"><a href="/appraisal-calculators/claim-gap-fee-estimator/"><small>Calculator</small><span>Claim Gap &amp; Fee Estimator</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/appraisal-calculators/rcv-acv-holdback-worksheet/"><small>Calculator</small><span>RCV / ACV &amp; Holdback Worksheet</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/appraisal-calculators/roof-square-pitch-calculator/"><small>Calculator</small><span>Roof Square &amp; Pitch Calculator</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-in"><div class="box box-call"><h2 class="box-h">Talk to an appraiser</h2><p>A free first conversation about your estimate — and a 